\section{Plotting corrections and interpretation}
The forest-plot diamond spans the reported confidence interval of the pooled
fixed-effect estimate. Its width is not a sample-size marker. Study squares
continue to reflect relative sample sizes. Funnel confidence limits use
$1.96\,SE$ on the same horizontal scale as the estimates.

For discrete-time effects, each curve uses the full drift matrix of its study
or moderator condition. Time ranges are numeric start/end/positive-step
triples, for example \texttt{c(10,12,1)} or \texttt{c(0,2,0.5)}.
Axis labels are placed at the actual time values. Power plots display all
available computed lags within the requested bounds, including fractional
lags; requesting a different step does not recompute power.

Multiple forest or funnel plots receive distinct filenames. Files are written
inside \texttt{activeDirectory}, which must already exist. PNG exports also
work from noninteractive graphics devices. Categorical moderator labels retain
signed, multi-digit values. Continuous moderator curves use the selected
moderator and its retained drift-parameter ordering; incomplete mappings are
reported rather than recycled.

Some older example objects contain only a summary drift table. When that table
can be mapped unambiguously to matrix cells, plotting reconstructs population
or reference point estimates and explicitly reports this limitation. Such
curves are not reconstructed study-specific heterogeneity. Missing or
ambiguous interval mappings must be supplied before plotting study curves.

